fat_parent is a native DOS regression for the directory-parent contract
fixed by AROS PR #2092. It needs no CPU emulator or application-specific
runtime. Build it explicitly with:
  make test-fat-parent-rename

On an expendable mounted FAT volume TEST:, run:
  fat_parent ROOT TEST:FatParent

The program creates the first unused TEST:FatParent-1 through -8 fixture,
plus its -Moved and -Root siblings. It does not overwrite existing objects
or remove the resulting fixtures. ROOT must resolve directly under the
volume root. The locked volume node must identify FAT12, FAT16 or FAT32;
DOS's generic Info.id_DiskType alone cannot distinguish FAT from other
handlers. The fixture is explicit rather than an automatic filesystem test.

It checks complete NameFromLock paths and ParentDir identity after moving
a directory from staging to the volume root, then to another parent and
back. It checks refusal of a cycle, replacement of a different existing
directory with the same leaf name, and a file used as a destination parent.
A case-only rename of the same entry remains allowed. A parent or refusal
failure stops the program before further potentially invalid renames.

BASELINE observes only the historical failure on an expendable volume
using a pre-fix handler:
  fat_parent ROOT TEST:FatParent BASELINE
It expects ParentDir error 205 and the incomplete NameFromLock result
after the first cross-parent move, then stops before refusal probes.

Each invocation returns zero only when its checks pass and prints:
  fat_parent: N checks, M failed
Normal complete execution currently performs 56 checks. Run the Shell and
actual Run invocations separately, retaining both return codes and the Run
completion marker. The fixtures are deliberately left for inspection.
On FAT32 the final root-parent link is encoded as zero; inspect raw links
only in a stopped scratch image, never on a live mounted filesystem.
